.TH GETES 3 "8086 Only"
.SH NAME
getesb, getesw, putesb, putesw \- read/write to ES memory
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B "char getesb(addr)"
.B "char *addr;"
.sp
.B "getesw(addr)"
.B "int *addr;"
.sp
.B "putesb(val, addr)"
.B "int *addr;"
.B "char val;"
.sp
.B "putesw(val, addr)"
.B "int *addr;"
.B "int val;"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
These functions transfer bytes or words between the 8086 Data Segment
(DS) and the Extra Segment (ES).
The DS is normal user program data
space, while the ES is special purpose data space.
The
.B phys
and
.BR sdata (2)
system calls manipulate the ES register, to map it, for example, to the
graphics display or to a data area common to several processes.
The
.BR getesb() ,
.BR putesb() ,
etc. functions then allow a program to
.B read
and
.B write
data from and to the extra segment.
.PP
.B getesb()
takes as an argument a 16 bit address (type
.BR *char )
in the ES and
returns the byte value
.RB ( char )
at that location.
.B putesb()
takes two arguments:
a 16 bit address
.RB ( *char )
and a single byte
.RB ( char )
value, and places
the value at that address in the ES.
The functions
.B getesw()
and
.B putesw()
provide identical capabilities for word
.RB ( int )
transfers.
.PP
If a
.B phys
or
.B sdata
call has not been done, the ES is identical to the normal
DS.
In this case, the functions listed here are not likely to be useful,
since normal memory to memory transfers are most easily done using
standard pointer operations.
.SH "SEE ALSO"
phys(2), sdata(2)
.sp
